Cloudflare Access applications stop being a Terraform resource and become a
Crossplane managed resource reconciled from this repo.

## What this chart installs

| Object | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `crossplane` subchart 2.4.2 | core controller; its init container installs the Crossplane CRDs (the chart ships none) |
| `Provider` `provider-cloudflare-zero` | `ghcr.io/jomakori/provider-cloudflare-zero:v0.4.2-jomakori.4` — the fork build of the upstream provider. Upstream publishes amd64 only; this index carries native `linux/amd64` **and** `linux/arm64` plus the package extensions. |
| `Function` patch-and-transform / auto-ready | Crossplane 2 compositions are Pipeline-only, so the transforms live in a function |
| `ExternalSecret` + `ProviderConfig` | `CF_API_TOKEN` from Doppler (`devops/svc_cloudflare`) as the `{"api_token": "..."}` credentials blob the upjet provider expects |
| `CompositeResourceDefinition` `CloudflareAccessApp` | platform API: `spec.accountId`, `spec.host`, `spec.sessionDuration`, optional `spec.policies` |
| `Composition` | one `TrustAccessApplication` per XR: name and domain from the XR name/host, `type: self_hosted`, policies passed through |

## Turning it on

```yaml
# services/argocd-appset/values.yaml
crossplaneAccess:
  accountId: "<CF_ACCOUNT_ID>"   # not a secret
services:
  crossplane:
    enable: true                 # wave 3: controller, provider, XRD, Composition
  cloudflareAccess:
    enable: true                 # wave 7: one XR per private host
```

Both flags ship `false`. Enabling `cloudflareAccess` makes
`services/argocd-appset` inject one `apps.<service>.host` parameter for every
service with `gateways.enable_private: true`, so a service still opts in with a
single flag and gets the Cloudflare Access app plus the Istio virtual host.

## Verifying

```bash
helm template crossplane services/helm/crossplane --namespace crossplane-system \
  | grep -E "^kind: (Provider|Function|CompositeResourceDefinition|Composition|ProviderConfig)"

kubectl get providers.pkg.crossplane.io,functions.pkg.crossplane.io
kubectl get cloudflareaccessapps.platform.maklab.net
kubectl get trustaccessapplications.zero.upjet-cloudflare.upbound.io -o \
  jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.status.atProvider.aud}{"\n"}{end}'
```

`status.atProvider.aud` is the audience the Access application was issued. It
is the value the Istio gateway has to accept.

## Known limitation (follow-up)

The Istio `RequestAuthentication` still takes its audience list from the
Terraform-generated `accessAudienceTag` (`CF_ACCESS_AUDS` in Doppler), because
Helm cannot render a value that only exists in another resource's status. Until
an aggregation step exists, a host moved to Crossplane needs its AUD added to
that list by hand — or the gateway keeps validating the Terraform-issued
audiences only. Tracked separately; this chart deliberately does not touch the
Terraform path yet.

Second known gap: `allowedIdps` is left empty on purpose, so the Access
application uses the account's default identity provider (Google). Terraform
pinned the Google IdP id explicitly; if a second IdP is ever added, the XR
schema needs that field.
